    "evidence_text": "The output of an LSTM at a time step $t$ is computed as follows: $\nh_t = o_t \\otimes \\tanh (c_t)\n$  where $\\otimes $ denotes element-wise multiplication.\n\nIn our methods, we rely on the last state $h_k$ to represent the vector $\\vec{v}_p$ of\n\nThe vector $\\vec{v}_{xy}$ is then fed into a logistic regression layer whose target is the class label associated with the pair $(x,y)$ .\n\nInspired by the supervised distributional concatenation method in Baroni:12 and the integrated path-based and distributional method for hypernymy detection in Shwartz:16, we take into account the patterns and distribution of target pairs to\n\nand the distributed method (Section \"Distributed Baseline\" ).\n\nTo apply the SP method to our data, we make use of the pre-trained SP embeddings with 500 dimensions.\n\nRegarding the low precision in the two proposed models, we sampled randomly 5 pairs in each population: true positive, true negative, false positive, false negative.\n\nAcknowledgements\tWe would like to thank Michael Roth for helping us to compute the results of the R&SiW model on our dataset.",
